According to the repository's general rules, when working with file contents in Go, we should avoid converting []byte to string for search and replace operations to prevent unnecessary memory allocations. We can use the bytes package directly on the byte slice instead.



        Suggested change






            	lines := strings.Split(string(content), "\n")



            	var newLines []string



            	for _, line := range lines {



            		if strings.HasPrefix(line, "version:") {



            			newLines = append(newLines, fmt.Sprintf("version: %s", newVersion))



            			continue



            		}



            		newLines = append(newLines, line)



            	i := slices.IndexFunc(lines, func(line string) bool { return strings.HasPrefix(line, "version:") })



            	if i < 0 {



            		return fmt.Errorf("no version found in %s", path)



            	}



            	return os.WriteFile(path, []byte(strings.Join(newLines, "\n")), 0644)



            	lines[i] = "version: " + newVersion



            	return os.WriteFile(path, []byte(strings.Join(lines, "\n")), 0644)



            lines := bytes.Split(content, []byte("\n"))



            i := slices.IndexFunc(lines, func(line []byte) bool { return bytes.HasPrefix(line, []byte("version:")) })



            if i < 0 {



            	return fmt.Errorf("no version found in %s", path)



            }



            lines[i] = []byte("version: " + newVersion)



            return os.WriteFile(path, bytes.Join(lines, []byte("\n")), 0644)







References

When working with file contents in Go, avoid converting []byte to string for search and replace operations to prevent unnecessary memory allocations. Use the bytes package (e.g., bytes.Contains, bytes.ReplaceAll) to perform operations directly on the byte slice.

